{"kind":"assert","text":"Ruling on fc038fd7 for the cutover head (05d618e2).\n\nChoose (c). `usesPerEntry` stays 128 and keeps its count of today.\nA birth use that the rule adds does not count against it.\n1. The bound on added births needs no new tunable number. Each\n   added birth belongs to a foreign sequence that has one or more\n   later uses in the same entry. So the added births never exceed\n   the later uses, and the later uses never exceed 128. State that\n   bound in the note. Check it at the charge site as an invariant\n   with a typed refusal, and name the refusal to me if it needs a\n   new word.\n2. An entry that already uses the first entry of a foreign\n   sequence adds no birth for that sequence. That one use serves\n   as the birth, and it counts against `usesPerEntry` as it does\n   today.\n3. The byte caps charge the birth bytes in full: the bytes of one\n   entry, the retained bytes, the observation cap, and the two\n   platform v17 budgets. Those caps are the real limit. Put the\n   measured numbers in the note, with the worst case of 128 later\n   uses from 128 sequences. If the worst case cannot fit a byte\n   cap, the entry is refused by that cap. Do not raise a byte cap\n   in this head.\n4. The validator's static bound on the uses of one entry must\n   agree with the runtime. A definition that validation admits\n   must not meet a runtime refusal from the birth count alone.\n\nThe two lane facts are accepted. Carry the birth bytes through\n`Fetched` and the judgment copies with no added read.\n\nQ-V1 is settled as you report. Q-C1 is accepted with the reason\n`malformed-summary`. Add one sentence to the note that names the\ncheck which refuses a wrong genesis that is a valid digest, with\nits file. If no check refuses it, stop and tell me.\n\nNF-4 landed as main e7db3872. I deploy it now, before the LOG-1\ngate. Do not start a gate until my END act.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}